    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d40">

                <pb facs="0168" n="52" xml:id="pg52"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 893.00/724.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Chargé d’Affaires to the Secretary of
                        State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 352.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1911-10-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 26, 1911</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In continuation of my No. 349 of October
                    11, 1911,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d40-f1"/> I have the honor to report that * * *
                    taking a general survey of the Empire one cannot but admit that the present
                    Manchu Dynasty is face to face with the gravest crisis in its history. Unless a
                    reconciliation can be effected there is at hand either a division of the Empire
                    or the entire overthrow of the Manchus. If Yuan Shih-Kai takes the leadership
                    and is successful in winning back the disaffected provinces by the introduction
                    of a reformed government, the dynasty may be saved. Otherwise there seems but
                    little hope of recovering control of the south except by the assistance of a
                    foreign power. * * * The Cabinet is without a definite policy except that of
                    endeavoring to persuade Yuan Shih-Kai to save the country. The Court appears to
                    be terror-stricken and princes are preparing for flight. * * * The consuls have
                    carefully refrained from any acknowledgment of the revolutionary authorities,
                    but since the Imperial authorities have fled they do not consider it possible to
                    avoid all intercourse. The doyen of the diplomatic corps in circulating the
                        telegram<anchor n="2" xml:id="d40-f2"/> expressed the opinion that the
                    consuls could not avoid holding intercourse with General Li on matters affecting
                    the security of foreign life and property, and that they should be allowed a
                    discretionary power of deciding on the spot what questions fall within this
                    category.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.],</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">E. T. Williams.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
